<script lang="ts">
  // Rendered from components/slug/index.md — regenerate with bin/generate-site-pages, do not edit.
  const intro: string = "<h1>HeatmapChartWithYearOfDays</h1>\n<p>A headless wrapper for a year-of-days heatmap: one cell per day of one year, laid out in week columns and weekday rows, with cell colour encoding the value of each day. It specialises <code>HeatmapChart</code> (same shell, same data-table sibling) and adds the two facts the layout needs, <code>year</code> and <code>weekStart</code>. Use it for a per-day measure across a year, such as contributions, visits, appointments or readings per day.</p>\n<p><strong>Status:</strong> experimental — added 2026-10-22 from the chanhdai.com <code>github-contributions</code> review (<a href=\"https://github.com/LilyDesignSystem/lily-design-system/blob/main/spec/chanhdai-lessons/index.md\">chanhdai-lessons</a>); implemented and unit-tested in all nine headless catalogs, not yet exercised in composed flows.</p>\n<p>The component renders a <code>&lt;figure&gt;</code> holding a <code>&lt;div class=&quot;heatmap-chart-with-year-of-days-graphic&quot; role=&quot;img&quot; aria-label&gt;</code> around an inline <code>&lt;svg&gt;</code> that the consumer draws, names the figure with <code>aria-label</code> (from <code>label</code>), and lets the consumer reference a longer description or a real data <code>&lt;table&gt;</code> through <code>aria-describedby</code>. The component draws nothing and ships no scales, colours or animation; it exists to give every chart in the catalog the same accessible, stylable shell.</p>\n";
  const usage: string = "<h2>Usage</h2>\n<pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"language-svelte\">&lt;HeatmapChartWithYearOfDays label=&quot;Activity per day in 2026&quot; year={2026} weekStart={1} aria-describedby=&quot;heatmap-chart-with-year-of-days-data&quot;&gt;\n  &lt;svg viewBox=&quot;0 0 100 100&quot;&gt;…&lt;/svg&gt;\n&lt;/HeatmapChartWithYearOfDays&gt;\n&lt;table id=&quot;heatmap-chart-with-year-of-days-data&quot;&gt;…&lt;/table&gt;\n</code></pre>\n";
  const rest: string = "<h2>Props</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Prop</th>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Default</th>\n<th>Description</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>class</code></td>\n<td>string</td>\n<td><code>&quot;&quot;</code></td>\n<td>Appended to the base class</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>label</code></td>\n<td>string (required)</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Accessible name</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>year</code></td>\n<td>number (required)</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>The calendar year shown; reflected as <code>data-year</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>weekStart</code></td>\n<td>number (required)</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>First weekday row, 0 = Sunday … 6 = Saturday; reflected as <code>data-week-start</code>. No default: it is locale-dependent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>children</code></td>\n<td>slot (required)</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>The consumer-drawn inline <code>&lt;svg&gt;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>...restProps</code></td>\n<td>HTML attributes</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Spread onto the root <code>&lt;figure&gt;</code>, e.g. <code>aria-describedby</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2>Layout contract</h2>\n<p>The consumer draws the grid; the component names it and carries the two numbers the drawing needs. The grid has seven weekday <strong>rows</strong> and one <strong>column</strong> per week, one cell per day of <code>year</code>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>row(d) = (weekday(d) − weekStart + 7) mod 7</code>, with <code>weekday</code> 0 = Sunday … 6 = Saturday, so the first row is the weekday <code>weekStart</code> names.</li>\n<li><code>column(d) = floor((dayOfYear(d) − 1 + row(1 January)) / 7)</code>, with <code>dayOfYear(1 January) = 1</code>. The first column holds 1 January; the grid has 53 or 54 columns, and the first and last columns are partly empty.</li>\n<li>365 cells, or 366 in a leap year; empty positions draw nothing.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><code>shared/year-of-days-layout.ts</code> implements this once (<code>yearOfDaysCells(year, weekStart)</code> returns <code>{ date, dayOfYear, column, row }</code> for every day; <code>columnCount</code> and <code>daysInYear</code> are alongside), in UTC calendar dates so a time zone cannot move a day. It is a pure module a drawing routine copies in, like the other <code>shared/</code> modules; the component itself does not call it.</p>\n<p><code>weekStart</code> has no default because the first day of the week depends on the reader&#39;s locale (Sunday, Monday and Saturday are all in use): the consumer passes it, from <code>Intl.Locale#getWeekInfo()</code> or its own setting. Month and weekday labels, and the legend, come from the consumer through <code>Intl</code>; the component supplies no strings.</p>\n<h2>Accessible alternative</h2>\n<p>Colour must not be the only carrier of the value (WCAG 1.4.1), and 365 unnamed cells are not an accessible chart. Supply a <code>dataTable</code>: a real <code>&lt;table&gt;</code> with one row per day, or per week or month when the consumer summarises, listing the date and the value. Do not rely on a <code>&lt;title&gt;</code> inside each cell: the graphic is <code>role=&quot;img&quot;</code>, so its children are presentational and those titles are never exposed.</p>\n<h2>Keyboard Interactions</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>None. The chart is a single image to assistive technology.</li>\n<li>A referenced data table follows native table behaviour.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>ARIA</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><code>role=&quot;img&quot;</code> exposes the chart as one image (its children are presentational)</li>\n<li><code>aria-label</code> provides the accessible name</li>\n<li><code>aria-describedby</code> (consumer-supplied) references the description or data table</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>When to Use</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>A per-day measure across one year, where the reader looks for streaks, gaps and seasons</li>\n<li>When the chart needs the catalog&#39;s standard accessible shell and a stable class hook</li>\n<li>When a real data table accompanies the drawing as the accessible alternative</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>When Not to Use</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Use <code>HeatmapChart</code> — when the two dimensions are not a calendar year (weekday by hour, for example)</li>\n<li>Use <code>LineChart</code> — when the trend matters more than the pattern of individual days</li>\n<li>Use <code>DataTable</code> — when readers need exact values and not a pattern</li>\n<li>Use <code>CalendarYearTable</code> or <code>CalendarMonthTable</code> — when readers pick or read specific dates, not scan a pattern</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Headless</h2>\n<p>This component decides semantics only: the figure element, the image role and the name. It decides no geometry, scale, colour, legend or motion.</p>\n<h2>Styles</h2>\n<p>Target <code>.heatmap-chart-with-year-of-days</code> for the figure and style the supplied <code>&lt;svg&gt;</code> from consumer CSS. No default styles are included.</p>\n<h2>Testing</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><code>year</code> and <code>weekStart</code> reach the figure as <code>data-year</code> and <code>data-week-start</code></li>\n<li>Renders a <code>&lt;figure&gt;</code> with class <code>heatmap-chart-with-year-of-days</code> and a <code>.heatmap-chart-with-year-of-days-graphic</code> child with <code>role=&quot;img&quot;</code></li>\n<li><code>label</code> sets <code>aria-label</code></li>\n<li><code>aria-describedby</code> and other rest props reach the figure</li>\n<li>The consumer svg renders inside the figure</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Advice</h2>\n<p>Fetching the per-day data, and any caching of it, is the consumer&#39;s job; the component takes the finished drawing. Pass the grid <code>&lt;svg&gt;</code> as children. Colour alone must never carry the value (WCAG 1.4.1): supply the same numbers as a real <code>&lt;table&gt;</code> and point <code>aria-describedby</code> at it.</p>\n<p>Because <code>role=&quot;img&quot;</code> makes descendants presentational, never put interactive controls inside the figure; place legends and toggles next to it.</p>\n<h2>Implementation Notes</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Renders <code>&lt;figure class=&quot;heatmap-chart-with-year-of-days {class}&quot; data-year data-week-start&gt;</code> containing the children</li>\n<li>The consumer supplies the <code>&lt;svg&gt;</code> (and any legend or caption markup)</li>\n<li><code>restProps</code> — including <code>aria-describedby</code> — spread onto the <code>&lt;figure&gt;</code></li>\n<li>No internal state, no drawing, no data handling</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Data table alternative (added 2026-10-05)</h2>\n<p>The graphic is wrapped in <code>&lt;div class=&quot;heatmap-chart-with-year-of-days-graphic&quot; role=&quot;img&quot; aria-label&gt;</code>; <code>role=&quot;img&quot;</code> is <strong>not</strong> on the <code>&lt;figure&gt;</code> any more. The optional <code>dataTable</code> (a snippet in Svelte, a prop in React, a named slot in Vue, a projected <code>[dataTable]</code> element in Angular, a <code>DataTable</code> render fragment in Blazor, <code>params.dataTable</code> in Nunjucks, a <code>slot=&quot;data-table&quot;</code> child in Web Components) renders the accessible table in <code>&lt;div class=&quot;heatmap-chart-with-year-of-days-data-table&quot;&gt;</code>, a <strong>sibling</strong> of the graphic and never inside it: <code>role=&quot;img&quot;</code> makes its descendants presentational, so a table inside would be invisible to assistive technology. Without a data table the wrapper is not rendered (except Angular, noted above).</p>\n<h2>Related components</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><code>heatmap-chart</code> — the general grid this specialises</li>\n<li><code>calendar-year-table</code></li>\n<li><code>scatter-chart</code></li>\n<li><code>data-table</code></li>\n<li><code>calendar-month-table</code></li>\n<li><code>line-chart</code></li>\n<li><code>graphic-block</code> — chart wrapper with title and notes</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>References</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Element/figure\">MDN figure element</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.w3.org/WAI/tutorials/images/complex/\">W3C WAI: Complex images</a></li>\n</ul>\n<hr>\n<p>Lily™ and Lily Design System™ are trademarks.</p>\n";
